Low-Cost Water Filtration Project
1. Project Title
Low-Cost Water Filtration Using Sand, Gravel and Charcoal
2. Introduction
Water is essential for life, but dirty water may contain soil, dust and other visible impurities. This project demonstrates a simple, low-cost filtration method using gravel, sand and charcoal.
The filter helps improve the clarity of water, but it does not make contaminated water safe for drinking. Filtered water should not be consumed unless it is properly disinfected and tested.
3. Aim
To build a simple water filter using easily available materials and compare the clarity of water before and after filtration.
4. Materials Required
Plastic bottle
Gravel/pebbles
Clean coarse sand
Clean fine sand
Activated charcoal
Cotton or clean cloth
Two transparent glasses
Dirty water
Measuring cup
Optional: Arduino Uno
Optional: turbidity sensor
Jumper wires
5. Working Principle
The filter works in layers:
Dirty water → Gravel → Coarse sand → Fine sand → Charcoal → Cotton → Filtered water
Gravel: Removes larger particles.
Coarse sand: Traps medium-sized particles.
Fine sand: Removes smaller suspended particles.
Charcoal: Can reduce some odors and certain dissolved substances.
Cotton/cloth: Helps prevent filter material from passing into the collected water.
6. Procedure
Take a clean plastic bottle.
Cut off the bottom of the bottle.
Turn the bottle upside down.
Place cotton or clean cloth near the bottle opening.
Add a layer of charcoal.
Add fine sand above the charcoal.
Add coarse sand.
Add gravel at the top.
Place a glass below the bottle opening.
Slowly pour dirty water into the filter.
Collect the filtered water.
Compare the water clarity before and after filtration.
